#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312538 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a58dad1-934e-5a45-915a-76e3b2021887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312540} {:answer/id #uuid "ba85e739-dd4f-52a8-85f9-ba7ec51f974c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312541} {:answer/id #uuid "065ca941-e234-5a92-b802-2932bbf44834" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312542} {:answer/id #uuid "06c683a2-2d0e-5c01-9e87-86a4e05dae88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312543} {:answer/id #uuid "9b1e8526-0a84-5038-aa10-071bae65c8d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312544}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a58dad1-934e-5a45-915a-76e3b2021887" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312540} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0cec1d2-d868-5876-ad9d-c032c64b9583" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312539}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4fd2bf14-4068-54c2-b1dc-152d783a9acf" :question/math-academy-id "q-113899" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/42fd32f22d25ed1d26d48ca8d4f1f5f454bcca67c68febaec066fa3b8bde0783.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave down *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by noticing that at $x=a$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=c$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f6e6c7b869268eba113690776b0aa07713ff12adb675e22e11406c4a13f0e7b2.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=c,$ the graph is concave down ($⌢$) at $x=c$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3289f80-ff41-5e82-bea9-cf2888f39163" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312547} {:answer/id #uuid "c2cdd6ec-d9fd-503b-8106-0608ae55490d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-2)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312548} {:answer/id #uuid "2509630c-d4e3-566a-9fb6-448f121263c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312549} {:answer/id #uuid "4d6b1b58-d4b4-567b-aa8a-d6dfdc3f36b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312550} {:answer/id #uuid "71977f26-b2ed-5872-8a7c-5e294851b4a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-2)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2509630c-d4e3-566a-9fb6-448f121263c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312549} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e7e3c1e-8e37-595e-a5c5-9c8a55a0a48a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312546}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08a439ff-480c-55d5-8674-962aa9f75a76" :question/math-academy-id "q-141937" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/583cde62650a6940389d807eee0ffeabdafcbb0da7b8d416af88e3c4d1b7bd71.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,0].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave down." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved downwards like a frown ($⌢$) over the intervals $(-2,-1)$ and $(-1,0)$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/ce57fc818eebd0cc5007e80a54b49d613eeb2823eefc98e946862ff9f7b68e0b.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312552 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e3b4b0b9-8711-5097-9eeb-4324a7565a0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312554} {:answer/id #uuid "f57f611a-5f5c-521f-bd6c-a4b9061cf7d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312555} {:answer/id #uuid "fdf4db48-262e-5199-8558-fd9936a87244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312556} {:answer/id #uuid "c0455006-d925-5c03-bd87-00275f668cba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312557} {:answer/id #uuid "723760f4-d87c-55fd-940d-d621100bfaa5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312558}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf4db48-262e-5199-8558-fd9936a87244" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312556} :answer-field/id #uuid "231ae621-13be-5af8-a53b-18ffa9b6ce4a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312553}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e7fc324b-e0cb-50c8-8439-3aa8d497d2c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-142065" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/de53aa4414f0c535562e0464b773928e505a98bea01afe5a12b2ec9b154b0b59.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)=0?$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is zero when $x$ is a stationary point (the tangent line is horizontal).\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that only $x=b$ and $x=d$ are stationary points.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/c7a00e3b25a9fc1a9a589234d0beb421330b58cfe69d3e745f432c3f9be09485.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=b$ and $x=d,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=d$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312559 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a67f347a-30a5-5169-af2c-9a800be84e46" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Concavity and the Sign of the Derivative at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cfa31fa7-5fae-5327-b68f-fc01392e3266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312561} {:answer/id #uuid "f825ec48-726d-5666-81a7-250762531d40" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312562} {:answer/id #uuid "6eed0e9e-4b46-5ea7-a1b6-07141b51ff2a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312563} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc17287-cc6c-54c7-986f-79dc1f9c4988" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=b" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312564} {:answer/id #uuid "1c7664b0-4825-5ce2-bb14-850460649f62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=a" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfa31fa7-5fae-5327-b68f-fc01392e3266" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=c" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312561} :answer-field/id #uuid "7d0fccec-52fd-5f53-ab17-0593bb1bfcf8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312560}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "77b3c6ac-2128-5535-a888-4c9030741d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-49003" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/03b4d6fea89eb695e0919aed4a82b1d25ff5e296338381192b5236f5295bef12.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. For which of the following values of $x$ is $f(x)$ concave up *and* ${f}^{′}(x)<0?$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by noticing that at $x=b$ the graph has a corner point, which implies that the derivative doesn't exist there.\n\nThe derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ is negative when $f(x)$ is decreasing. From the given graph, we see that only $x=c$ and $x=e$ are on decreasing parts of the graph.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/f33639f6a54a2e0f35c52b684356ed832047ee538b8651e9ca9f75a542d366a9.png)\n\nThe concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nOf the points $x=c$ and $x=e,$ the graph is concave up ($⌣$) at $x=c$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186312566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "51c15d7a-cd9c-58df-a750-97df0b695c64" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Intervals of Concavity From the Graph of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3059c31a-419b-577d-9e2a-c9dc33926436" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,0)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312568} {:answer/id #uuid "2ccbfa6d-7e4b-5201-b891-4a109137db60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(1,3)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312569} {:answer/id #uuid "499c9371-0b12-58d1-b38d-0d9b698b3e77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312570} {:answer/id #uuid "14cbe873-6978-523a-8a60-07648f81a130" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-1,1)$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312571} {:answer/id #uuid "27bedf57-8c65-5cfb-a3b5-0b59d2fc61e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312572}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "27bedf57-8c65-5cfb-a3b5-0b59d2fc61e5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3)$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312572} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c805d20-58ea-50b5-b987-72d200e6f61b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b1beed24-efd2-587e-8b4f-b044374db684" :question/math-academy-id "q-51432" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/0c8969fae3e21c8e5f5019509636e2307211921d742e6f067d9ee1c50499541b.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=f(x),$ defined on $[-3,3].$ Find the intervals on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up." :question/worked-solution "The concavity of a function refers to the direction in which its graph curves.\n\n- If the graph curves upward, like a \"smile\" ($⌣$), then the graph is said to be *concave up*.\n\n- If the graph curves downward, like a \"frown\" ($⌢$), then the graph is said to be *concave down*.\n\nIt's helpful to remember the mnemonic \"concave down like a frown.\"\n\nWe can see that the function is curved upwards like a smile ($⌣$) over the intervals $(-3,-1)$ and $(1,3).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041640/assets/ae9ce6ca2597a4d5d1ca150c1e97d7c7a0a35f59fe5d6421b75203c6882d2052.png)"}]}
